 "workflows": [],
 "body": "## Objective\n\nWhat share of e-receipt (mailbox) spend goes to Steam, and how is that share trending year on year? Built as an exploration page for analysts covering PC gaming / Valve-exposed names.\n\n## Data locations\n\n### Inputs\n\n- **`SHARE_NIQ.SRS.ITEMS_EXTENDED`** (NIQ Slice, US), pulled 2026-11-18, complete to 2026-11-15. Steam lines: `merchant_name = 'Steam'`. Mailbox spend: all merchants except Amazon (its parsed volume jumps ~25x from Q4-24 and falls back in Q3-26).\n- **`SHARE_NIQ.SRS.FOX_TRANSACTION_ITEMS`** (NIQ Fox, UK, Germany, France, Italy, Spain), pulled 2026-11-18, complete to 2026-11-08. Steam lines: `merchant_name = 'steam'`. Deduplicated to one row per merchant, order and item (Fox repeats a line per industry tag).\n- Both: daily from 2020-01-01, priced lines only (`item_price > 0`), line value (price x quantity) up to 10,000 in local currency.\n- **Card source (Source toggle), US: `CER.CER_1415_MU_DAILY_SIGNAL`** (Consumer Edge MU aggregate, projected US card spend), pulled 2026-11-19, from 2021-04-01. Steam: `DAY_SYM_BRAND_CHANNEL_ALL` brand 12648 (STEAM). Card spend: `TOTAL_GEOGRAPHY_CHANNEL_DAY` rows with `subindustry_id = 'NA'` and `industry_name = 'ALL'`, summed over every metro area. The DISTRIBUTION channel is left out of both.\n- **Card source, EU5: `SHARE_FABLE.FABLE_SIGNAL.{UK,DE,IT,ES}_V2_TXN`, `FR_V3_TXN`**, pulled 2026-11-19, from 2020-01-01. Consumer accounts (`usertype = 'C'`), debits (`spendout < 0`), `txntype = 'CARD PAYMENT'`, up to 10,000. Steam by card descriptor (STEAMGAMES, STEAM PURCHASE, STEAM GAMES, WL *STEAM, KLARNA*STEAM, VALVE CORP). Fable's srs aggregate (`fable.common.machine_data_daily_enhanced_srs_agg`) has a `steam games` tag but no panel total; summing its tags breaks in 2026, when large merchants (e.g. Allianz in Italy) lose their tag.\n\n### Method\n\n1. **Steam spend types** (keyword rules in SQL on the lower-cased description, or the item after the last \"|\" for Fox, first match wins): wallet top-ups, hardware, subscriptions, in-game currency / passes / keys, premium editions, DLC / add-ons, games (everything else). Rules are `TYPE_RULES` in the notebook; it lists the largest items per type as a check. Last 12 months, US: games 54%, wallet 18%, in-game 10%, DLC 7%, editions 7%, hardware 4%, subscriptions 0.5%; EU5: games 66%, in-game 14%, DLC 9%, editions 6%, hardware 3%.\n2. **Share** = Steam spend / mailbox spend over a trailing window (1, 7, 28 or 91 days; sums, not an average of daily shares), per country in local currency (no FX).\n3. **Regions.** EU5 = population-weighted average of the five country shares; Combined = population-weighted average of all six (US 335m, UK 68.3m, DE 84.5m, FR 68.4m, IT 59.0m, ES 48.6m). Regions stop at their members' earliest last complete day.\n4. **Y/Y** = value on a day / value 364 days earlier - 1, so weekdays line up. EU5 and Combined Y/Y is of the population-weighted share.\n5. **Quarters** = share over each calendar quarter's days (current quarter to date), Y/Y against the same days shifted back 364 days.\n6. **Last complete day** per country: latest day whose mailbox spend is at least 75% of the median of the prior 28 days, and at most two days before the pull.\n7. **Fixed merchant cohort (Mailbox spend toggle):** per country, only merchants with priced spend in every calendar month Jan 2021-Oct 2026 (US 799, UK 393, DE 358, FR 530, IT 240, ES 201 merchants; 92-99% of mailbox spend). Removes the effect of merchants entering the parsed panel later. It moves quarterly share Y/Y by at most 2.4pp (US Q4-25), usually under 1pp.\n8. **Year chart:** one line per year on a Jan-Dec axis; year k back is shifted forward k x 364 days, so the gap between adjacent lines is the Y/Y. Nominal shows the share, Y/Y the share Y/Y.\n9. **Type tick boxes:** the share and Y/Y use the sum of the ticked types. Default: every type except wallet top-ups (top-ups fund later purchases that send their own receipts, so including them double counts).\n10. **Source toggle:** E-receipt (default) or Card. Card share = Steam card charges / all card spend, same windows, regions, Y/Y and quarters. The last 10 days of each card series are dropped while they settle. Card sees every charge to Steam (wallet top-ups included) but not purchases paid from wallet balance, and has no item detail, so with Card every type is ticked and locked, and the spend base is all merchants (the fixed cohort is e-receipt only). Card totals include groceries, fuel and bills, so card shares (~0.05-0.1%) sit below mailbox shares; compare the Y/Y. Since Apr 2024 the 91-day share Y/Y correlates 0.81 (US) and 0.68 (EU5) with e-receipts excl. wallet top-ups.\n\n### Outputs\n\n- **`s3://srsinvest-apps/s3-browser/personal/fintan-creedon/steam-share/steam.json`**: per country, daily arrays of Steam spend for each of the seven types and mailbox spend for all merchants and for the fixed merchant cohort (local currency) from 2020-01-01 to the country's last complete day, plus population weights, and a `card` block per country (daily Steam and total card spend, start and end). The page computes windows, shares, regions, Y/Y and the quarter table in the browser.\n\n## Routines and manual steps\n\n- **Who and when:** fintan.creedon@srsfund.com, by hand. Nothing schedules it.\n- **Steps:**\n  0. Card: `python card/pull_card_steam.py mu` and `python card/pull_card_steam.py eu` (set `STOP` to today; a few minutes on ANALYST_XL).\n  1. In the exploration repo, `steam/notebooks/build_steam_share.py`: set `PULL_END` to yesterday and `REFRESH = True` (or delete `data/*.parquet`).\n  2. `python build_steam_share.py`, then `jupyter nbconvert --to notebook --execute --inplace steam_share.ipynb` (~10 min on ANALYST_XL).\n  3. Section 5 writes `app/steam-share/steam.json` and checks the page's calculation against section 2.\n  4. Upload `steam.json` to the folder above with the SRS MCP `s3` tool (`upload_large`, with `if_match`).\n- **Check:** the page's stamp shows the new Slice, Fox and card end dates.\n\n## Good to know\n\n- **Fox lags Slice** by about a week and refreshes in bulk, so EU5 and Combined end earlier than the US.\n- **EU before 2021 is noise** (Fox had ~50k active panelists), so charts start in 2021 and Y/Y charts in 2022.\n- **Launch-driven swings.** Q4-25 was lifted by Battlefield 6 and ARC Raiders on Steam (~$1.2m of US Steam spend in 2 Oct-9 Nov 2025), so Q4-26 to date is down Y/Y against that base.\n- **Hardware.** Steam Machine and Steam Frame (2026) add hardware spend; untick Hardware to strip it out.\n- Slice lines are not deduplicated beyond what `ITEMS_EXTENDED` does, consistent with the video-game wallet share in `video_games/notebooks/aaa_franchise_trends.ipynb`.\n",
